/** @page mldataguide Matrix, DataFrame, and Pipeline Data

    @tableofcontents

    The core \c ml module is intentionally matrix-oriented and does not depend on
    the \c dataframe module.  This keeps the binary module small and usable in
    installations that do not need DataFrame APIs.  Use row matrices
    (\c list<list<float>>) or list-of-record hashes for direct model APIs, and
    use DataFrame conversion or DataProviderML processors when the source data is
    columnar.

    @par DataFrame to matrix example:
    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe
%requires ml

DataFrame::DataFrame df({
    "amount": (45.0, 52.0, 50000.0, 48.0),
    "hour": (10.0, 11.0, 3.0, 10.0),
});

list<auto> matrix = df.toMatrix(("amount", "hour"));

Qore::ML::StandardScaler scaler();
scaler.fitMatrix(matrix);
list<auto> scaled = scaler.transformMatrix(matrix);
    @endcode

    @par Shape-aware pipeline example:
    @code{.py}
%requires dataframe
%requires DataProviderML

DataFrame::DataFrame df({
    "amount": (45.0, 52.0, 50000.0, 48.0),
    "hour": (10.0, 11.0, 3.0, 10.0),
});

DataProviderML::QoreIsolationForestProcessor proc({
    "fields": "amount,hour",
    "window_size": 4,
});

hash<auto> events = proc.processRecordsShape(
    df, DataProviderShape::DataFrameBlock);
    @endcode

    \c DataProviderML processors also accept \c BufferColumns input,
    including @ref Qore::SQL::ColumnarResult "ColumnarResult" values
    produced by SQL queries.  Rectangular bulk outputs preserve
    \c DataFrameBlock or \c BufferColumns shapes when possible so downstream
    shape-aware processors can continue in columnar bulk mode; processors with
    naturally row-oriented or multi-output responses keep \c HashOfLists or row
    compatibility.
*/
